EFM sink, data mode: wait for a whole sector after a sync that lies near the
end of the buffer.

Data24ToRawSector::waitingForSync() drops the bytes before a sync pattern it
finds, then counts the error and padding flags of the next kRawSectorSize
bytes. When the sync lay less than a sector from the end of the buffer, fewer
than kRawSectorSize bytes are left, and the count reads past the end of
m_sectorErrorData and m_sectorPaddedData. With libstdc++ that read lands in
reserved capacity and goes unnoticed; macOS's hardened libc++ traps it
(SIGTRAP), within the first second of any data-mode decode.

Waiting for the rest of the sector is enough: the next call finds the sync
again at position 0.

Applied by flake.nix to the pinned decode-orc until decode-orc itself has it.

--- a/orc/plugins/stages/common/efm-decode/decoders/dec_data24torawsector.cpp
+++ b/orc/plugins/stages/common/efm-decode/decoders/dec_data24torawsector.cpp
@@ -387,6 +387,14 @@
     // Keep only data from syncPatternPosition onwards
     eraseFront(static_cast<size_t>(syncPatternPosition));

+    // The sync may lie less than a sector from the end of the buffer. Its
+    // error and padding flags cannot be counted until the rest of the sector
+    // arrives, so wait for it: the next call finds the sync again at
+    // position 0.
+    if (m_sectorData.size() < efm::kRawSectorSize) {
+      return WaitingForSync;
+    }
+
     ORC_LOG_DEBUG(
         "Data24ToRawSector::waitingForSync(): Possible sync pattern found in "
         "sectorData at position: {} discarding {} bytes",
